s1: 1 <composition>
	why=Reading text aloud needs the same chunking and block-finding logic whichever engine speaks it, and locating a reader-chosen start point needs the same kind of pure text math.
	build=Text is split into speakable sentence chunks, EPUB/PDF content is turned into an ordered list of readable pieces, and a selection or a tap is turned into a sentence index inside one of those pieces.
	evidence=Each helper is a pure function with a doctest-style example in its own docstring.
	code=|
		- [`splitSentences`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=splitSentences kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_1: 1.1 Split text into sentence chunks <leaf>
		why=A screen reader speaks better in sentence-sized pieces than one long paragraph, and no engine here chunks its input for the caller.
		description=Splits on sentence-ending punctuation, then hard-wraps any piece still longer than 280 characters at the nearest space.
		build=`splitSentences(text, max)` returns an ordered array of chunks no longer than `max`.
		evidence=Its docstring example turns `"One. Two!  Three?"` into `['One.', 'Two!', 'Three?']`.
		code=|
			- [`splitSentences`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=splitSentences kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_2: 1.2 Collect readable DOM blocks <leaf>
		why=`playEpub` (4.3) needs one flat, ordered list of the paragraph-like elements in an EPUB chapter, with nested duplicates removed.
		description=Selects `p,h1..h6,li,blockquote,dt,dd,figcaption,pre,td,th` and drops a block that contains another block, so nested text is read once.
		build=`collectBlocks(doc)` returns the leaf block elements of a document in reading order.
		evidence=Called from `playEpub` (4.3) on the EPUB iframe document, and again by `nextBlocks` (4.7) each time it re-checks whether a new block has appeared.
		code=|
			- [`collectBlocks`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=collectBlocks kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_3: 1.3 Locate the block at an anchor <leaf>
		why=Reading must start from the block the reader can currently see, not from the top of the chapter.
		description=Returns the index of the first block at or after a given DOM node, using document position when the node is not itself a block.
		build=`blockIndexFrom(blocks, node)` returns `-1` when `blocks` is empty and no block follows `node`.
		evidence=Feeds `playEpub` (4.3), which starts its walk from `reader._visibleAnchorEl()` whenever no explicit start cursor (4.5) was given.
		code=|
			- [`blockIndexFrom`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=blockIndexFrom kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_4: 1.4 Turn PDF text items into text <leaf>
		why=`pdf.js` returns text as a list of positioned items, not a string; `splitSentences` (1.1) needs a string.
		description=Joins each item's `str`, inserting a newline after an item whose `hasEOL` flag is set and a space otherwise.
		build=`pdfItemsToText(items)` returns one string per page, ready for `splitSentences`.
		evidence=Feeds `pdfSentences` (4.9), which caches one call per rendered PDF page for `playPdf` (4.4) and `stepCursor` (4.6).
		code=|
			- [`pdfItemsToText`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=pdfItemsToText kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_5: 1.5 Locate the sentence at a text offset <leaf>
		why=`playFrom` (4.10) must skip the sentences already spoken before a chosen start point, not restart the whole block.
		description=Splits the block's text the same way `splitSentences` (1.1) does, then walks the resulting chunks until the given character offset falls inside one.
		build=`sentenceIndexAt(text, offset)` returns the index of the `splitSentences()` chunk that holds `offset`.
		evidence=Its docstring example turns `('One. Two! Three?', 6)` into `1`.
		code=|
			- [`sentenceIndexAt`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=sentenceIndexAt kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_6: 1.6 Convert a DOM point to a character offset <leaf>
		why=A selection or a caret lookup gives a `(node, offset)` DOM point; `sentenceIndexAt` (1.5) needs a plain character offset counted from the start of the block's own text.
		description=Builds a `Range` from the start of the block element to `(node, offset)` and measures its stringified length; returns `0` if the `Range` cannot be built.
		build=`textOffsetIn(el, node, offset)` returns the character offset of `(node, offset)` inside `el.textContent`.
		evidence=Feeds `playFrom` (4.10) directly, which calls it once per jump to compute the skip offset passed to `sentenceIndexAt` (1.5).
		code=|
			- [`textOffsetIn`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=textOffsetIn kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_7: 1.7 Locate the DOM point under a tap <leaf>
		why=`playFromPoint` (4.11) only has viewport coordinates from a touch event; the sentence helpers (1.5, 1.6) need a DOM point.
		description=Uses `caretRangeFromPoint` where the runtime supports it, else `caretPositionFromPoint`; returns `null` when neither does.
		build=`caretAt(doc, x, y)` returns `{node, offset}` or `null`.
		assumption=Unknown (needs your test): `caretRangeFromPoint`/`caretPositionFromPoint` support was not verified across every browser this reader targets.
		code=|
			- [`caretAt`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=caretAt kind=Function focus=changed}
s2: 2 <composition>
	why=Android's WebView has no `window.speechSynthesis`, so speaking on Android needs the platform's own `TextToSpeech` reached through a JavaScript bridge.
	build=A `JsBridge` method starts or stops speech, sets the output volume for the next utterance, and reports the read-aloud session's state; a lazily created `TextToSpeech` instance speaks one utterance at a time, and its result is reported back into the page.
	evidence=`window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak`/`ttsStop`/`ttsSetVolume`/`ttsSessionState` exist on the bridge and every utterance resolves to exactly one `window.__codexaTtsEvent` call.
	assumption=Unknown (needs your test): confirm the app builds and speaks on a device before relying on it.
	code=|
		- [`MainActivity` JsBridge](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=217:239}
	n2_1: 2.1 Declare TTS_SERVICE package visibility <leaf>
		why=Android 11+ hides other packages from an app unless it declares which intents it needs to query, and `TextToSpeech` must find an installed engine package.
		description=Adds a `<queries>` block naming the `android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE` intent action.
		build=The manifest grants visibility into installed TTS engines on API 30+.
		evidence=Without it, `TextToSpeech(this){...}` can silently find no engine on a fresh API 30+ install.
		code=|
			- [`AndroidManifest.xml`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=7:12}
	n2_2: 2.2 Expose ttsSpeak/ttsStop to JavaScript <leaf>
		why=`androidEngine` (3.1) in the page needs a synchronous-looking call into native code, and Android's JS bridge only accepts primitive arguments and returns nothing.
		description=`@JavascriptInterface` methods that hop to the UI thread and call `speakTts`/stop the current utterance.
		build=`ttsSpeak(id, text, rate, lang)` and `ttsStop()` are callable as `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak(...)` from `androidEngine` (3.1).
		evidence=`androidEngine.speak` calls `bridge.ttsSpeak` inside a try/catch and resolves `'error'` if the call throws.
		code=|
			- [`ttsSpeak`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=218:221}
			- [`ttsStop`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=229:232}
	n2_3: 2.3 Speak with lazy init and one pending utterance <leaf>
		why=Creating a `TextToSpeech` engine is asynchronous and should happen once, not on every utterance; a call that arrives before init finishes must not be dropped.
		description=Speaks immediately once the engine is ready, at the volume `ttsSetVolume` (2.7) last set; otherwise replaces any earlier pending utterance (reporting it `stopped`) and starts the engine at most once.
		build=`speakTts(id, text, rate, lang)` keeps at most one queued utterance and lazily owns the `TextToSpeech` instance.
		evidence=A second call while the engine is still initializing reports the first call's id as `stopped`, matching the "one pending utterance" comment above it.
		code=|
			- [`speakTts`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=251:266}
	n2_4: 2.4 Finish init and listen for utterance progress <leaf>
		why=`speak()` only reports through callbacks; the bridge (2.2) needs each callback turned into exactly one event per utterance id.
		description=On successful init, sets the engine's audio attributes (7.2 shares the same attributes for audio-focus requests), plays the one pending utterance, and installs an `UtteranceProgressListener` that maps `onDone`/`onError`/`onStop` to `sendTtsEvent` (2.5). On failure, shuts the engine down and reports `error`.
		build=`onTtsInit(status)` leaves `tts` either usable and listened-to, or `null`.
		evidence=`sendTtsEvent` fires exactly once per utterance id, from whichever branch of the listener the engine takes.
		code=|
			- [`onTtsInit`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=268:288}
	n2_5: 2.5 Report an utterance event back to JavaScript <leaf>
		why=`androidEngine` (3.1) resolves its `speak()` promise from `window.__codexaTtsEvent`, so the native side needs a way to call back into the page.
		description=Builds a small JS call with the utterance id and event name and runs it through `WebView.evaluateJavascript`.
		build=`sendTtsEvent(id, event)` calls `window.__codexaTtsEvent(id, event)` in the page, guarded by `&&` so a page without the handler does nothing.
		evidence=`androidEngine`'s `pending` map (3.1) resolves the matching promise the moment this call lands.
		code=|
			- [`sendTtsEvent`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=383:386}
	n2_6: 2.6 Release TextToSpeech on destroy <leaf>
		why=A `TextToSpeech` engine holds a system-service connection that leaks if the activity is destroyed while it is open.
		description=Shuts the engine down and clears the reference before the WebView itself is destroyed; `onDestroy` also releases the media session (7.6).
		build=`onDestroy()` calls `tts?.shutdown()` ahead of its existing WebView teardown.
		evidence=No test exercises `onDestroy`; the user's own manual app-close check is the evidence for this leaf.
		code=|
			- [`onDestroy` TTS teardown](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=438:440}
	n2_7: 2.7 Set output volume for the next utterance <leaf>
		why=The reader's volume slider (5.11) changes what the reader hears immediately, but `TextToSpeech.speak` only takes a volume per call, not a standing setting.
		description=Clamps the given volume to `0..1` and stores it; `speakTts` (2.3) reads the stored value into `KEY_PARAM_VOLUME` on every call.
		build=`ttsSetVolume(volume)` is callable as `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSetVolume(volume)` from `androidEngine` (3.1).
		evidence=`androidEngine.speak` calls `bridge.ttsSetVolume(volume)` immediately before `bridge.ttsSpeak`, guarded so an older APK without the method is skipped.
		code=|
			- [`ttsSetVolume`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=224:227}
	n2_8: 2.8 Expose ttsSessionState to JavaScript <leaf>
		why=Headset media keys (7) only work while the native media session mirrors the read-aloud session, and the page is the one place that knows whether a session is active or playing.
		description=`@JavascriptInterface` method that hops to the UI thread and updates the media session (7.1).
		build=`ttsSessionState(active, playing)` is callable as `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSessionState(active, playing)` from `androidEngine` (3.1).
		evidence=`androidEngine.session` calls `bridge.ttsSessionState(active, playing)` on every controller state change, guarded so an older APK without the method is skipped.
		code=|
			- [`ttsSessionState`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=234:238}
s3: 3 <composition>
	why=Two runtimes can speak: Android WebView through a native bridge, and iOS WKWebView/browsers through `speechSynthesis`. The controller (4) should not know which one it has.
	build=Both engines expose the same `speak(text, {rate, volume, lang}) -> Promise<'done'|'stopped'|'error'>` and `stop()` shape; `pickEngine` chooses one at controller creation.
	evidence=`createTts` (4) calls `pickEngine()` once and never branches on engine name again.
	code=|
		- [`pickEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=pickEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_1: 3.1 Android engine adapter <leaf>
		why=`window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak` resolves through an out-of-band callback (2.5), not a return value, so it needs wrapping into the shared Promise-based engine shape; headset keys (7) also need to know whether this engine is currently active or playing.
		description=Keeps a `pending` map from utterance id to its `resolve` function; `window.__codexaTtsEvent` (set here) looks the id up and resolves it. `speak` sets the device volume through `bridge.ttsSetVolume` (2.7) before every call. `session(active, playing)` forwards the read-aloud session's state to `bridge.ttsSessionState` (2.8) so headset media keys keep working (7.1); both calls are guarded for an older APK build that lacks the method.
		build=`androidEngine(win, bridge).speak(text, opts)` resolves once `sendTtsEvent` (2.5) reports that utterance's id; `androidEngine(win, bridge).session(active, playing)` reports session state.
		evidence=`stop()` resolves every still-pending promise as `'stopped'` before clearing the map, so a stopped engine never leaves a caller waiting.
		code=|
			- [`androidEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=androidEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_2: 3.2 Web engine adapter <leaf>
		why=iOS WKWebView and desktop/mobile browsers expose `window.speechSynthesis` directly; no native bridge is needed there.
		description=Wraps one `SpeechSynthesisUtterance` per call, setting its `rate` and `volume`; `unlock()` speaks an empty utterance so iOS treats the first real utterance as inside the same user gesture.
		build=`webEngine(synth).speak(text, opts)` resolves `'done'`, or `'stopped'` for an interrupted/canceled utterance, or `'error'` otherwise.
		assumption=Unknown (needs your test): whether iOS's silent (mute) switch silences `speechSynthesis` inside a WKWebView is unconfirmed on-device.
		code=|
			- [`webEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=webEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_3: 3.3 Pick an engine for this runtime <leaf>
		why=The controller (4) is created once per rendition and must not re-detect the runtime on every utterance.
		description=Prefers `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak` when present, then `window.speechSynthesis`, else no engine.
		build=`pickEngine(win)` returns an engine object or `null`; `createTts` (4.1) treats `null` as "read-aloud unavailable".
		evidence=`_tts.available` (used by 5.2's header-button visibility) is exactly `!!engine` from this pick.
		code=|
			- [`pickEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=pickEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
s4: 4 <composition>
	why=One controller owns the play/pause/stop state, the block-by-block or page-by-page read, the cursor a pause resumes from, and where in the book a run starts.
	build=`createTts(reader, opts)` returns `{available, active, playing, stop, toggle, prevSentence, nextSentence, prevPage, nextPage, refresh, playFrom, playFromPoint, onRelocated}` bound to one `CXReader` instance.
	evidence=Toggling `btn-tts` (5.2) starts and pauses audible speech with the highlighted block tracking the page; selecting text or tapping while reading jumps that same run to a chosen sentence; pausing and resuming continues from the same sentence.
	code=|
		- [`createTts`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus=changed}
	n4_1: 4.1 Session state: active, playing, and the cursor <leaf>
		why=A reader can pause without ending the session, and headset/Bluetooth keys (7) and the bar's own controls (5.12) all need one shared idea of "reading is on, but not making sound right now" that survives across a pause.
		description=`active` is true from the first `start()` until `stop()` or the natural end of the book; `playing` is true only while an utterance is in flight or about to be. `cursor` is the sentence being read — `{el, sentence}` for EPUB, `{page, sentence}` for PDF — and a `run` counter is bumped on every `start()`/`pause()`/`stop()`, so every loop and `await` in `playEpub`/`playPdf` (4.3, 4.4) checks its own captured `run` value before acting again.
		build=`start(from)` begins a new run at `from` or the current cursor; `pause()` bumps `run` and stops the engine but keeps `cursor`; `stop()` bumps `run`, stops the engine, and clears `cursor`; `toggle()` calls `pause()` when `playing`, else `start(cursor)`.
		invariant=Pausing never clears the cursor: `toggle()` and the media-key handler (7.5) both resume with `start(cursor)`, so the next spoken sentence is the one that was interrupted, not the block's first sentence or the visible anchor.
		evidence=Acceptance example: pause mid-sentence, then press play again (button, bar, or a Bluetooth headset) — the same sentence is read again from its start, not the next one.
		code=|
			- [`start`/`pause`/`stop`/`toggle`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function start(from = null) {]" context=20}
	n4_2: 4.2 Restart reading after a user page turn <leaf>
		why=A page turn the reader makes themselves should not be silently ignored, and a page turn this module makes to keep pace should not restart the same block.
		description=Ignores `cx-relocated` while the session is not active, while `selfTurn` is true (this module is mid page-turn), or while the sentence being read is still on screen (`cursorVisible()`); otherwise clears the cursor and, if a run was playing, restarts it from the new visible page.
		build=`onRelocated()` is the whole rule; `cursorVisible()` checks PDF page visibility or EPUB element visibility depending on `reader._isPdf`; `turn(fn)` (used by 4.3/4.4/4.8) is what sets `selfTurn` around this module's own `reader.next()`/`goToSpineItem()`/`reader.prev()` calls.
		invariant=(a) TTS never turns a page the reader did not ask for, except to keep the block it is speaking on screen; a reader-driven relocation that moves the read sentence off screen restarts reading from the newly visible page.
		decision=Restarting from the new page trades mid-sentence continuity for a simple rule: no resume position is tracked across a manual turn that moves the read sentence off screen.
		evidence=Acceptance example: open an EPUB in the Android app, tap read-aloud, hear the first visible paragraph, and see the page turn only when speech reaches the next page.
		code=|
			- [`onRelocated`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[onRelocated() {]" context=6}
			- [`cursorVisible`/`turn` self-turn guard](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[function cursorVisible() {]" context=10}
	n4_3: 4.3 Read an EPUB block by block <leaf>
		why=An EPUB chapter is longer than one screen, so reading it must walk blocks across page turns and, in continuous mode, across appended chapters; and it must be able to start mid-chapter at a chosen point instead of always at the visible anchor.
		description=Starts at `from.el` (4.10) when given, skipping to `from.sentence`; otherwise finds the visible anchor (1.3). Turns pages under the guard (4.2, via `showBlock`) until the next block is visible, highlights it with `cx-tts-active`, and speaks its sentences (1.1) in order; when the block list runs out, `nextBlocks` (4.7) re-collects blocks (1.2) at a chapter or continuous-append boundary.
		build=`playEpub(myRun, from)` runs until `myRun !== run` (4.1), an error, or `nextBlocks` (4.7) reports the end of the book.
		evidence=Acceptance example: the highlighted paragraph advances one at a time and the page turns only when the next block is off screen.
		code=|
			- [`playEpub`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function playEpub(myRun, from) {]" context=20}
			- [`showBlock` page-turn loop](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function showBlock(el, myRun) {]" context=6}
	n4_4: 4.4 Read a PDF page by page <leaf>
		why=A PDF has no DOM blocks; its text comes from `pdf.js` one page at a time, and a scanned page has no text layer at all.
		description=Walks `reader.spine` from `from.page` (4.10) or the current page, skipping to `from.sentence`; reads each page's cached sentences (4.9, 1.1) in order and stops, reporting no readable text, after 3 consecutive empty pages.
		build=`playPdf(myRun, from)` runs until `myRun !== run` (4.1), an error, no-text, or the end of the spine.
		evidence=`opts.onNoText()` fires only after 3 consecutive pages with zero sentences, so one blank page does not stop the read.
		code=|
			- [`playPdf`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function playPdf(myRun, from) {]" context=20}
	n4_5: 4.5 Read-aloud session drives the Android media session <leaf>
		why=Headset and Bluetooth media keys (7) only work while Android's own media session reflects whether reading is active and playing; something inside the controller has to keep that in step without every leaf knowing about the bridge.
		description=`emit()` runs on every state change (`start`, `pause`, `stop`) and calls both `engine.session(active, playing)` (3.1) and `opts.onState({active, playing})` (5.4), so the native media session (7.1) and the reader UI (5.10) update from the same one place.
		build=`emit()` is the single seam the rest of the controller calls after changing `active`/`playing`.
		evidence=`onState` toggles the bar's `open`/`playing` classes (5.10) in the same call that reports session state to Android (7.1), so the two never disagree about whether a session is active.
		code=|
			- [`emit`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[const emit = () => { engine?.session?.(active, playing); opts.onState?.({ active, playing]" context=1}
	n4_6: 4.6 Step the cursor across sentences and pages <leaf>
		why=The bar's previous/next-sentence controls (5.12) must be able to move the cursor by one sentence even across a block or PDF-page boundary, without re-running the whole block/page walk.
		description=For a PDF cursor, moves the sentence index and crosses into the neighbouring page (using the cache, 4.9) when it runs past either end of the current page's sentences. For an EPUB cursor, moves the sentence index and crosses into the neighbouring block (1.2) when it runs past either end of the current block's sentences; stepping before the first sentence of the book stays on sentence `0` instead of returning `null`.
		build=`stepCursor(delta)` returns the new cursor, the unchanged cursor at the very start of the book, or `null` past the very end.
		evidence=`prevSentence`/`nextSentence` (api) call `stepCursor` then `start(cursor)`; `nextSentence` falls back to `start()` with no cursor when stepping forward runs out of blocks, so it can pick up the next chapter the same way `playEpub` (4.3) does.
		code=|
			- [`stepCursor`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function stepCursor(delta) {]" context=20}
	n4_7: 4.7 Find the next blocks to read, waiting once for a continuous-mode append <leaf>
		why=Reading an EPUB in continuous (scrolled) mode must not falsely conclude "end of book" while the reader's own async chapter-append is still in flight.
		description=Re-collects blocks (1.2) and looks for one after the last block already read; if the last block is no longer attached to the document, starts over from the first block of the freshly collected list instead of assuming a page turn moved nothing. If a page turn does not reveal a new block and the rendition is in continuous mode, waits once for `reader._maybeAppendNextChapter()` before treating a further non-moving turn as the true end of the book.
		build=`nextBlocks(myRun, blocks)` returns `{blocks, idx}` for the next readable block, or `null` at the end of the book.
		evidence=A continuous-mode chapter append that is still running when the reader reaches its bottom does not end the read; a plain page turn that reveals nothing (the real end of the book) still ends it after the single wait.
		assumption=Unknown: whether continuous mode ever actually detaches the previously-read block from the document, and whether restarting from the fresh list's first block lands on the sentence the reader expects, is still unverified against every continuous-mode re-render path; worth a manual check in continuous mode specifically.
		code=|
			- [`nextBlocks`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function nextBlocks(myRun, blocks) {]" context=18}
	n4_8: 4.8 Turn a page without moving the cursor <leaf>
		why=The bar's "read from previous/next page" buttons (5.12) must turn one page and start reading from wherever that page begins, discarding the old cursor instead of resuming near it.
