	n7_2: 7.2 Request and release audio focus while playing <leaf>
		why=Reading should not talk over another app's audio, and should not keep exclusive audio focus once nothing is being spoken.
		description=Requests `AUDIOFOCUS_GAIN` only while playing; on a full loss of focus, abandons the request; on any loss (including transient, such as a short notification sound), pauses reading through `sendTtsMedia` (7.4) instead of stopping it outright.
		build=`requestTtsFocus()`/`abandonTtsFocus()`, called from `updateTtsMediaSession` (7.1) and `onDestroy` (2.6).
		evidence=`sendTtsMedia("pause")` is called from the focus-change listener itself, using the same media action path a physical pause button uses.
		code=|
			- [`requestTtsFocus`/`abandonTtsFocus`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=5f0d08e..b6b8933 show=lines lines=358:376}
	n7_3: 7.3 Keep media-key routing on this app between utterances <leaf>
		why=Android routes media-button events to whichever process most recently played audio; `TextToSpeech` audio plays inside the engine's own process, not this app's, so between utterances the keys can drift to whatever music app played last.
		description=Builds a 200ms silent mono `AudioTrack` with the same audio attributes as speech and plays it from this app's own process immediately after a play state change, so this app is again "the process that most recently played audio" without the reader hearing anything.
		build=`playSilence()`, called from `updateTtsMediaSession` (7.1) whenever `playing` is true.
		evidence=No automated test exercises this; the user's on-device confirmation that headset keys keep working across a pause/resume is the evidence for this leaf specifically.
		code=|
